// WHY: A function parameter `p` shadowed inside an `if` body that has NO suspension
// within that if body (the top-level `wait` is outside the if). Under Option A
// (design/concurrency.md § ShadowsCrossingLocal), any nested `let p` in a suspending
// function is rejected — the frame-slot system keys slots by NAME, and every
// continuation state's reload_params_from_frame overwrites cg.locals["p"] with the
// slot pointer. Even a non-crossing inner shadow would install the wrong alloca at
// the next suspension. The per-binding-ID lifting path is roadmap M3c.
//
// This fixture MUST produce a clean compile error (exit 1) with the shadow
// diagnostic. It must NOT produce any ICE text.
// test-ratchet: R6 flipped this to compile (param_shadow_has_genuine_crossing);
// R7 reverts to conservative reject (param_has_nested_let_shadow, Option A).
// Rationale: R6's non-crossing compile path silently miscompiled `f(7)` → printed
// garbage instead of 7 in the code-reviewer's repro. Conservative reject is the
// correct safe answer per design/concurrency.md ShadowsCrossingLocal.
function worker(p: int) -> nothing {
  if (p > 0) {
    let p: int = 42
    print(p.toString())
  }
  wait sleep(5)
  print(p.toString())
}
function entrypoint() -> nothing {
  worker(10)
}
